# A wire value cannot hold a list

Found binding `query_path_info`. `PathInfo` carries what a store knows
about a path, and two of the fields Nix gives are sets:

    StorePathSet references;   // what this path points at
    std::set<Signature> sigs;  // who vouched for it

Neither was in the binding, and this is why.

## Where it stopped

`_wire_fields` names a field's type, and the codec read it in
`wire.value_to_msg` / `value_from_msg`. Both handled exactly two
kinds: a scalar, which is assigned, and another wire value, which is
filled in place. A `list[T]` field fell through to `setattr(msg,
fname, val)`, and a repeated protobuf field cannot be assigned - not
even an empty one.

The RPC layer already knew how to do this. `encode` and `decode` carry
every arm - scalar, map, list, value, proxy - and `list_to_msg` and
`list_from_msg` already carried `query_all_valid_paths`. The two
dispatches had simply drifted.

## What it became

A wire value's field is put the way an rpc field is put. Not a third
arm added to a second copy: the second copy is gone, and
`value_to_msg` calls `encode` while `value_from_msg` calls `decode`.

One arm a wire value may never take is the proxy. It is refused at
build time - a wire value is rebuilt from its parts on the far side,
and a proxy is exactly the thing with no object there - so the codec
passes a callable that says so rather than a handle resolver.

`check_wire_contract` reads a container field's ELEMENT type, through
the same `list_value` and `map_value` the schema builder uses. It also
refuses `?` on one: a repeated field has no presence, so an absent one
IS an empty one and the marker would promise a distinction that cannot
exist.

Nothing in `grpc_schema` changed. `_add_field` already built a list as
`LABEL_REPEATED`, for method parameters, and the value message was
already built through it.

## The other half: a parameter

`add_to_store` and `add_path_to_store` both take `references` in C++
and both pinned it to empty. That is the same type in the other
direction, and it had an extra question the return did not: what does
it DEFAULT to?

Answered. `None`, and a container is the one type whose default may be
None. A repeated field has no presence PROBLEM - an absent one and an
empty one mean the same thing - so None crosses as nothing and the far
side reads back the empty container it means. `default_source` takes
the parameter's type and answers by it.

`[]` is what a reader expects instead, and it is now refused with its
own message. It reads back as itself, so the literal check would have
let it through; it is a mutable default, and every generated surface
would carry its own copy of one the binding has once.

The two halves need each other. An empty list proves the ENCODING on
its own, because assigning even `[]` to a repeated field raises - but
only a non-empty one proves the decoding, and only the parameter could
produce one hermetically. Nix does not scan an added path for
references; it is told them.

## Done

`PathInfo.references()` and `PathInfo.sigs()`. `references` is the
field that makes a store path a graph rather than a name: a closure is
the transitive reading of it. Both are sets in Nix and vectors in the
shim, because that is what a pxd can declare, and a set is sorted, so
the order a caller sees is the store's own.

`nix::Signature` is a key name and raw bytes rather than a string. Its
own `to_string` writes the `<key-name>:<base64>` spelling every Nix
tool prints, so the binding does not invent one.

Both add methods take `references: list[StorePath] | None = None`. It
is typed, so a base name that only reads like a store path is refused
before libstore sees it.

## Also missing, noticed here

`nix::Store::toStorePath(path)` answers "which store path contains
this file", which is a different question from `parse_store_path`.
`sys.executable` is inside a store path and is not one, and the live
test had to use `sys.prefix` to avoid the gap.

Closed by 042.
